{
  "id": "80793",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Nuclear Stability",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A nucleus is stable if it cannot be transformed into another configuration without adding energy from the outside. Of the thousands of nuclides that exist, about 250 are stable. A plot of the number of neutrons versus the number of protons for stable nuclei reveals that the stable isotopes fall into a narrow band. This region is known as the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "band of stability"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Region on a graph of proton number versus neutron number containing all the stable (nonradioactive) nuclides; also called the "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "zone"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ", "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "belt"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ", or "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "valley of stability"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1619037638",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1461536087",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " (also called the belt, zone, or valley of stability). The straight line in the graph below represents nuclei that have a 1:1 ratio of neutrons to protons (n:p ratio). Note that the lighter stable nuclei, in general, have equal numbers of protons and neutrons. For example, nitrogen-14 has seven protons and seven neutrons. Heavier stable nuclei, however, have increasingly more neutrons than protons. For example, iron-56 has 30 neutrons and 26 protons, an n:p ratio of 1.15, whereas the stable nuclide lead-207 has 125 neutrons and 82 protons, an n:p ratio of 1.52. This is because larger nuclei have more proton–proton repulsions and require larger numbers of neutrons to provide compensating strong forces to overcome these electrostatic repulsions and hold the nucleus together."
              }
            ],
            "id": "l4378p4vfn43pnk",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A graph is shown where the x-axis is labeled “Number of neutrons, open parenthesis, n, close parenthesis” and has values of 0 to 180 in increments of 10. The y-axis is labeled “Number of protons, open parenthesis, Z, close parenthesis” and has values of 0 to 120 in increments of 10. A green shaded band of varying width, labeled “Radioactive,” extends from point 0 on both axes to 178 on the y-axis and 118 on the x-axis in a linear manner. The width of this band varies from 8 to 18 units in width according to the x-axis measurements. A blue line in a roughly zig-zag pattern runs through the middle of the shaded band and stops at 128 on the y-axis and 82 on the x-axis. This line is labeled “Nonradioactive.” An unlabeled, black, solid line extends from point 0, 0 to 120, 120 in a linear manner.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "This plot shows the nuclides that are known to exist and those that are stable. The stable nuclides are indicated in blue, and the unstable nuclides are indicated in green. The solid line is the line where "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "n = Z"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "cp277jvrw9f3xdj",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "fjc93wnk1isx86r",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/fd/fd8eeecc5acd2062460691d113bece67/nuclear_graph.jfif",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The nuclei to the left or the right of the band of stability are unstable and exhibit radioactivity. They change spontaneously (decay) into other nuclei that are either within or closer to the band of stability. These nuclear decay reactions convert one unstable isotope (or "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "radioisotope"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A radioactive isotope of an element; it has an unstable nucleus and emits radiation as it decays to a more stable form."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1227696254",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3572741019",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ") into another, more stable, isotope. We discuss the nature and products of this radioactive decay later in this module."
              }
            ],
            "id": "i3pqd7ocfv1pkny",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "3939689097",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "activity_id": 78723,
            "id": "1605385274",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          },
          {
            "activity_id": 79778,
            "id": "3321766232",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          },
          {
            "activity_id": 80290,
            "id": "4112793821",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          }
        ],
        "id": "3908944303",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "didigetthis",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Several observations can be made regarding the relationship between the stability of a nucleus and its structure.  Isotopes of elements with atomic numbers greater than 83 are unstable/radioactive. Nuclei with even numbers of protons, neutrons, or both are more likely to be stable. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "4274339555",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "xrd3t9zu9cmnk71",
                "text": "Stable Isotopes of Select Elements"
              }
            ],
            "id": "bbs6upo5p7n9th6",
            "type": "h5"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Nuclei with certain numbers of nucleons, known as "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "magic numbers"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A number of protons or neutrons corresponding to a complete shell of nuclear energy levels and a particularly stable nucleus; common magic numbers are 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, and 126."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "532191811",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3979853258",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", are stable against nuclear decay. These numbers of protons or neutrons (2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, and 126) make complete shells in the nucleus. They are similar in concept to the stable electron shells observed for the noble gases.  "
              }
            ],
            "id": "ghpiqhmcly86l31",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "sxlx93ygyl40bh3",
                "text": "Stability and Binding Energy per Nucleon"
              }
            ],
            "id": "84yowknuscf41g9",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The relative stability of a nucleus is correlated with its binding energy per nucleon, the total binding energy for the nucleus divided by the number of nucleons in the nucleus. For instance, we saw in the example on the previous page that the binding energy for a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " nucleus is 28.4 MeV. The binding energy per nucleon for a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " nucleus is therefore:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "oe2pozb7wa4t50h",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "57lm2e68cnozpme",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9any1v9gb74xvcw",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\frac{28.4\\;\\mathrm{MeV}}{4\\;\\mathrm{nucleons}}\\;=\\;7.10\\;\\mathrm M\\mathrm e\\mathrm V/\\mathrm n\\mathrm u\\mathrm c\\mathrm l\\mathrm e\\mathrm o\\mathrm n",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In the following example, we learn how to calculate the binding energy per nucleon of a nuclide on the curve shown below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ji71e262z4kd4uy",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A graph is shown where the x-axis is labeled “binding energy per nucleon, open parenthesis, M e V, close parenthesis” and has values of 0 to 10 in increments of 1. The y-axis is labeled “Mass number” and has values of 0 to 260 in increments of 20. A line of best fit beginning at point 0, 0 is drawn through points “8, 5.5; 9, 7.3; 18, 7.1; 20, 7.5; 19, 7.9; 27, 7.8; 21, 8.1; 25, 8.4; 37, 8.6; 43, 8.8; 57, 8.6; 60, 8.9; 70, 9; 88, 8.8; 102, 8.9; 108, 8.5; 126, 8.7; 133, 8.8; 143, 8.2; 157, 8.1; 167, 8.2; 195, 7.9; 205, 7.9; 241, 7.3 and 255, 75. An upward-facing arrow near the bottom left of the graph is labeled “Fusion” while a left-facing arrow near the top right is labeled “Fission.”",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The binding energy per nucleon is largest for nuclides with a mass number of approximately 56."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ok4kn5ie3fr7a6a",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ukmtdc197ywijmi",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/84/84f7733b4026771983f0c2a0e7b28392/binding_energy_curve.jfif",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "901738060",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "3514426580",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
